{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 23, :comments ["<b>And Shem and Japheth took the garment.</b> Rashi explained: from this he merited the garment of tzitzit. And the Re'em raised a difficulty from what our Rabbis of blessed memory said (Sotah 17) that in the merit of ״if from a thread to a shoe strap״ (Genesis 14:23) they merited the thread of tekhelet. And it appears that this is no difficulty at all, for in the merit of covering with that garment they merited a garment, and in the merit of a thread they merited a thread — for a person cannot fulfill the commandment of tzitzit until the Holy One, blessed be He, first gives him a garment, as is found in Midrash Tanchuma, and the Yalkut brought it at the end of the book of Job on the verse ״Who has preceded Me that I should repay״ (Job 41:3) — who made tzitzit before I gave him a tallit? This is its plain sense. And by way of allusion, it appears to me as follows, since it is known that the commandment of tzitzit saves a person from illicit relations, as is found in Menachot (44a) in the incident of one who hired a prostitute for four hundred zuz and in the end was saved from the transgression by means of the tzitzit. And one who is saved from illicit relations is also saved from poverty, for ״he who tends prostitutes will lose wealth״ (Proverbs 29:3), and as our Rabbis of blessed memory said (Sotah 4a): whoever lies with a prostitute, in the end seeks a loaf of bread and does not find it. If so, tzitzit save from two things: from the sin of illicit relations and from poverty. And Israel merited these two things in the merit of two things: for in the merit of ״וַיִּקַּח שֵׁם וָיֶפֶת אֶת הַשִּׂמְלָה״ to cover their father's nakedness, they merited tzitzit, which save from forbidden relations. And in the merit of ״אִם מִחוּט וְעַד שְׂרוֹךְ נַעַל״ (Genesis 14:23), that he did not covet others' money, he merited a second reward drawn from the thread of tzitzit, which is salvation from poverty — for who is wealthy? One who rejoices in his lot. And in parashat Shelach (Numbers 15:38) it will be explained, with the help of HaShem, a reason for what our Rabbis of blessed memory said (Menachot 43b): tekhelet resembles the sea, and the sea resembles the sky, etc., along the lines of the midrash that is cited in the Yalkut of parashat Ha'azinu: Look at the heavens — perhaps they have changed their measure, etc., and likewise look at the sea, which does not depart from the boundary of measure, etc. And through seeing the color tekhelet one will remember the sky and the sea, and from them one will see and likewise act so as not to depart from the boundary of measure even by the full measure of a thread. And they merited this in the merit of ״אִם מִחוּט״ etc., that Abraham did not depart from the boundary of contentment even by the full measure of a thread — and this is a proper allusion."], :book :genesis, :voice :kli-yakar, :lang :en, :chapter 9, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:27:53.338457671Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}